**Recursive idea:** the smallest digit of n is the smaller of its last digit (`n % 10`) and the smallest digit of the rest (`n / 10`). Base case: a single digit is its own smallest digit.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int smallest(int n)
{
    int last, rest;
    if (n < 10)                    /* base case: one digit */
        return n;
    last = n % 10;
    rest = smallest(n / 10);       /* recursive call */
    return (last < rest) ? last : rest;
}

int main()
{
    int n;
    printf("Enter a number: ");
    scanf("%d", &n);
    if (n < 0) n = -n;
    printf("\nSmallest digit = %d\n", smallest(n));
    return 0;
}
```

**Trace for 58372:** smallest(5) = 5, then min(8,5) = 5, min(3,5) = 3, min(7,3) = 3, min(2,3) = **2**.

**Sample run** (input `58372`):
```
