|---|---|
| 8 5 7 2 | 2 5 7 8 |
| 7 3 0 1 | 0 1 3 7 |
| 8 5 3 2 | 2 3 5 8 |
| 9 4 2 1 | 1 2 4 9 |

### Logic
- Seats are stored in an array `seats[0..n-1]` (0 = free, 1 = occupied), accessed only through a pointer.
- `findSeat(seats, n, cur, checked)` is recursive:
  - **Base case 1:** if `checked == n`, every seat has been examined, return -1 (no seat).
  - **Base case 2:** if `*(seats + cur) == 0`, seat `cur` is free, return `cur`.
  - **Recursive step:** try the next seat in circular order `(cur + 1) % n` with `checked + 1`.
- The wrap-around `% n` makes the search circular (after the last seat it continues at seat 0).

### Program
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int findSeat(int *seats, int n, int cur, int checked)
{
    if (checked == n)                 /* all seats examined */
        return -1;
    if (*(seats + cur) == 0)          /* free seat found */
        return cur;
    return findSeat(seats, n, (cur + 1) % n, checked + 1);
}

int main(void)
{
    int n, i, req, seat;
    int *seats;

    printf("Enter total number of seats: ");
    scanf("%d", &n);
    if (n <= 0) return 1;

    seats = (int *)malloc(n * sizeof(int));
    if (seats == NULL) return 1;

    printf("Enter availability of each seat (0 = free, 1 = occupied):\n");
    for (i = 0; i < n; i++)
        scanf("%d", seats + i);

    printf("Enter requested seat number: ");
    scanf("%d", &req);
    if (req < 0 || req >= n)
    {
        printf("Invalid seat number\n");
        free(seats);
        return 1;
    }

    seat = findSeat(seats, n, req, 0);
    if (seat == -1)
        printf("No available seats\n");
    else
    {
        *(seats + seat) = 1;          /* mark as occupied */
        printf("Allocated seat number: %d\n", seat);
    }

    free(seats);
    return 0;
}
```

### Dry run (example from the question)
Seats `1 0 0 1 1`, request 3:
- seat 3 occupied, call with 4
- seat 4 occupied, call with (4 + 1) % 5 = 0
- seat 0 occupied, call with 1
- seat 1 free, return 1

Output: `Allocated seat number: 1`. With seats `1 1 1 1 1` the recursion stops after 5 checks and prints `No available seats`.

**Logic:** for $z_1 = a + ib$ and $z_2 = c + id$, the sum is $z_1 + z_2 = (a + c) + i(b + d)$: real parts are added together and imaginary parts are added together.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

struct complex
{
    float real;
    float imag;
};

struct complex add(struct complex x, struct complex y)
{
    struct complex r;
    r.real = x.real + y.real;
    r.imag = x.imag + y.imag;
    return r;
}

int main(void)
{
    struct complex c1, c2, sum;

    printf("Enter real and imaginary part of first number: ");
    scanf("%f %f", &c1.real, &c1.imag);
    printf("Enter real and imaginary part of second number: ");
    scanf("%f %f", &c2.real, &c2.imag);

    sum = add(c1, c2);

    if (sum.imag >= 0)
        printf("Sum = %.2f + %.2fi\n", sum.real, sum.imag);
    else
        printf("Sum = %.2f - %.2fi\n", sum.real, -sum.imag);
    return 0;
}
```

**Sample run**
```
Enter real and imaginary part of first number: 2.5 3
Enter real and imaginary part of second number: 1.5 -5
Sum = 4.00 - 2.00i
```
Check: $(2.5 + 1.5) + i(3 - 5) = 4 - 2i$.

