**Idea:** put the divisibility test inside the `for` condition, so the loop stops either at the first divisor or when `i * i > n`. After the loop, `n` is prime exactly when `n > 1` and no divisor was found (`i * i > n`). The result is printed with the conditional operator `?:`, so no `if` is used anywhere.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int n, i, prime;

    printf("Enter a number: ");
    scanf("%d", &n);

    /* stop at the first divisor, or once i*i exceeds n */
    for (i = 2; i * i <= n && n % i != 0; i++)
        ;

    prime = (n > 1) && (i * i > n);

    printf(prime ? "%d is a prime number\n" : "%d is not a prime number\n", n);
    return 0;
}
```

