**Idea:** keep two variables, `first` (largest) and `second` (second largest), and update them in one pass. Equal values are skipped so a repeated largest number is not taken as second largest.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <limits.h>

int main(void)
{
    int a[10], i, first = INT_MIN, second = INT_MIN;

    printf("Enter 10 numbers: ");
    for (i = 0; i < 10; i++)
        scanf("%d", &a[i]);

    for (i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
        if (a[i] > first) {
            second = first;          /* old largest becomes second */
            first = a[i];
        } else if (a[i] > second && a[i] != first) {
            second = a[i];
        }
    }

    if (second == INT_MIN)
        printf("No second largest number (all elements equal)\n");
    else
        printf("Second largest number = %d\n", second);
    return 0;
}
```

**Sample run:** input `5 17 3 89 42 89 11 60 7 25` gives `Second largest number = 60` (89 appears twice but is the largest).

