**Logic:** for Choice `p`, search downward from Num$-1$ for the nearest prime $M < $ Num; for Choice `N`, search upward from Num$+1$ for the nearest prime $M >$ Num. A number $x$ is prime if no $i$ with $2 \le i \le \sqrt{x}$ divides it.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int isPrime(int x)
{
    int i;
    if (x < 2) return 0;
    for (i = 2; i * i <= x; i++)
        if (x % i == 0) return 0;
    return 1;
}

int main(void)
{
    int num, m;
    char choice;

    printf("Enter Num (> 10): ");
    scanf("%d", &num);
    printf("Enter Choice (p or N): ");
    scanf(" %c", &choice);

    if (num <= 10) {
        printf("Num must be greater than 10\n");
        return 0;
    }

    switch (choice) {
    case 'p': case 'P':          /* nearest prime M < Num */
        m = num - 1;
        while (!isPrime(m)) m--;
        printf("Prime M (< %d) = %d\n", num, m);
        break;
    case 'N': case 'n':          /* nearest prime M > Num */
        m = num + 1;
        while (!isPrime(m)) m++;
        printf("Prime M (> %d) = %d\n", num, m);
        break;
    default:
        printf("Invalid choice\n");
    }
    return 0;
}
```

**Sample runs**

| Num | Choice | Output |
|---|---|---|
| 20 | p | Prime M (< 20) = 19 |
| 20 | N | Prime M (> 20) = 23 |
| 13 | N | Prime M (> 13) = 17 |

**Idea:** for any two numbers, $|a-b|$ is the gap between them. Adding the gap to the sum gives twice the larger; subtracting it gives twice the smaller.

$$\max(a,b) = \frac{(a+b) + |a-b|}{2}, \qquad \min(a,b) = \frac{(a+b) - |a-b|}{2}$$

No `if`, `?:`, `switch` or loop is needed; `abs()` comes from `<stdlib.h>`.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
int main()
{
    int a, b, max, min;
    printf("Enter two numbers: ");
    scanf("%d %d", &a, &b);
    max = ((a + b) + abs(a - b)) / 2;
    min = ((a + b) - abs(a - b)) / 2;
    printf("\nMaximum = %d\nMinimum = %d\n", max, min);
    return 0;
}
```

**Sample run** (input `12 25`):
```
Enter two numbers: 
Maximum = 25
Minimum = 12
```
