**Logic (trial division, no user-defined function):**
- Start with divisor `i = 2`.
- While `i` divides `n`, print `i` and divide `n` by `i` (this prints repeated factors like 2, 2).
- Then try the next `i`. Stop when `i * i > n`; if `n > 1` is left, it is itself a prime factor.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int n, i, first = 1;

    printf("Enter a number (>= 2): ");
    scanf("%d", &n);

    if (n < 2)
    {
        printf("No prime factors\n");
        return 0;
    }

    printf("Prime factors: ");
    for (i = 2; i * i <= n; i++)
    {
        while (n % i == 0)
        {
            printf(first ? "%d" : ", %d", i);
            first = 0;
            n = n / i;
        }
    }
    if (n > 1)                      /* remaining prime factor */
        printf(first ? "%d" : ", %d", n);
    printf("\n");
    return 0;
}
```

**Sample runs**

| Input | Output |
|---|---|
| 36 | `Prime factors: 2, 2, 3, 3` |
| 121 | `Prime factors: 11, 11` |
| 97 | `Prime factors: 97` |

(36 = 2 × 2 × 3 × 3. The question lists only "2, 2, 3"; the complete factorisation also contains a second 3.)

