### Idea
Only one item is ordered at a time, so only one of `RAM_SIZE` and `Energy_Efficiency_Ratio` is ever needed. Storing them in a **union** makes them share the same memory, so the record takes the size of the larger member instead of the sum. `ITEM_ID` and `COLOR_CODE` are common to both items, so they stay in the structure. A 1-byte `type` tag records which union member is valid; it fits in the padding after `COLOR_CODE`, so it costs no extra memory.

### Program
```c
#include <stdio.h>

struct item
{
    int  ITEM_ID;
    char COLOR_CODE;
    char type;                         /* 'L' = laptop, 'A' = air conditioner */
    union
    {
        int   RAM_SIZE;                /* laptop only */
        float Energy_Efficiency_Ratio; /* AC only */
    } u;
};

int main(void)
{
    struct item it;
    int choice;

    printf("Order: 1. Laptop  2. Air conditioner : ");
    scanf("%d", &choice);

    printf("Enter ITEM_ID: ");
    scanf("%d", &it.ITEM_ID);
    printf("Enter COLOR_CODE (single character): ");
    scanf(" %c", &it.COLOR_CODE);

    if (choice == 1)
    {
        it.type = 'L';
        printf("Enter RAM_SIZE (GB): ");
        scanf("%d", &it.u.RAM_SIZE);
    }
    else
    {
        it.type = 'A';
        printf("Enter Energy Efficiency Ratio: ");
        scanf("%f", &it.u.Energy_Efficiency_Ratio);
    }

    printf("\nItem details\nITEM_ID: %d\nCOLOR_CODE: %c\n", it.ITEM_ID, it.COLOR_CODE);
    if (it.type == 'L')
        printf("Item: Laptop\nRAM_SIZE: %d GB\n", it.u.RAM_SIZE);
    else
        printf("Item: Air conditioner\nEER: %.2f\n", it.u.Energy_Efficiency_Ratio);

    printf("Memory used by the record: %zu bytes\n", sizeof(it));
    return 0;
}
```

### Memory comparison (int = float = 4 bytes, typical compiler)

| Design | Size |
|---|---|
| One structure holding all four fields (ID, colour, RAM, EER) | 4 + 1 + 3 (padding) + 4 + 4 = 16 bytes |
| Structure with a union for RAM / EER (above) | 4 + 1 + 1 + 2 (padding) + 4 = **12 bytes** |

### Sample run
