**Key points:** `"w"` creates or truncates, `"a"` writes at the end without erasing, `"r"` reads; always check `fopen` for `NULL` and `fclose` every file.

### Logic

1. Read the 9 elements of the 3x3 board (each must be 0 or 2) and display the matrix.
2. Count $T0$ (number of 0s) and $T2$ (number of 2s). The move rule requires $T0 = T2 + 1$ or $T0 = T2 - 1$; otherwise print "invalid player movement".
3. Check the 8 lines: 3 rows, 3 columns, 2 diagonals. If all 3 elements of a line are equal, that value (0 or 2) wins.
4. If no line is complete, print "Match Draw".

### Program

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int b[3][3], i, j, t0 = 0, t2 = 0, winner = -1;

    printf("Enter 9 elements (0 or 2) of the 3x3 board:\n");
    for (i = 0; i < 3; i++)
        for (j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
            scanf("%d", &b[i][j]);
            while (b[i][j] != 0 && b[i][j] != 2) {
                printf("Only 0 or 2 allowed, re-enter: ");
                scanf("%d", &b[i][j]);
            }
        }

    printf("Board:\n");
    for (i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
        for (j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
            printf("%d ", b[i][j]);
            if (b[i][j] == 0) t0++;
            else              t2++;
        }
        printf("\n");
    }

    if (!(t0 == t2 + 1 || t0 == t2 - 1)) {
        printf("invalid player movement\n");
        return 0;
    }

    for (i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
        if (b[i][0] == b[i][1] && b[i][1] == b[i][2])      /* row i */
            winner = b[i][0];
        if (b[0][i] == b[1][i] && b[1][i] == b[2][i])      /* column i */
            winner = b[0][i];
    }
    if (b[0][0] == b[1][1] && b[1][1] == b[2][2])          /* main diagonal */
        winner = b[0][0];
    if (b[0][2] == b[1][1] && b[1][1] == b[2][0])          /* other diagonal */
        winner = b[0][2];

    if (winner != -1)
        printf("%d-You win the Match\n", winner);
    else
        printf("Match Draw\n");
    return 0;
}
```

### Dry run on the sample boards

| Board (row wise) | T0, T2 | Complete line | Output |
|---|---|---|---|
| 0 2 0 / 0 2 2 / 0 0 2 | 5, 4 | column 1 all 0 | **0-You win the Match** |
| 0 0 2 / 0 0 2 / 2 2 0 | 5, 4 | main diagonal all 0 | **0-You win the Match** |
| 0 0 0 / 0 2 2 / 2 0 2 | 5, 4 | row 1 all 0 | **0-You win the Match** |
| 0 2 0 / 2 2 0 / 0 0 2 | 5, 4 | none | **Match Draw** |
| 0 0 0 / 0 0 0 / 2 2 2 | 6, 3 | (not checked) | **invalid player movement** |

### Key points
- With 9 cells, the valid counts are (T0, T2) = (5, 4) or (4, 5); anything else is an illegal game.
- Every row, column and diagonal is tested with two equalities chained by `&&`.
- `winner` starts at -1 so that "no line found" is different from a winner of 0.

**Logic**

1. LSB (least significant byte) $=$ `n & 0xFF`.
2. Upper 4 bits $=$ `(LSB >> 4) & 0x0F`; lower 4 bits $=$ `LSB & 0x0F`.
3. Compare the two nibbles.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int n, i;
    unsigned char lsb, high, low;

    printf("Enter last two digits of roll no: ");
    scanf("%d", &n);

    lsb  = n & 0xFF;          /* least significant byte      */
    high = (lsb >> 4) & 0x0F; /* first (upper) 4 bits         */
    low  = lsb & 0x0F;        /* last (lower) 4 bits          */

    printf("LSB = ");
    for (i = 7; i >= 0; i--) {
        printf("%d", (lsb >> i) & 1);
        if (i == 4) printf(" ");
    }
    printf("\n");

    if (high == low)
        printf("Equal\n");
    else
        printf("Not equal\n");
    return 0;
}
```

