**Logic**
1. **Palindrome:** reverse the digits (`rev = rev*10 + n%10`, `n = n/10`) and compare with the original. Example: 121 reversed is 121, so it is a palindrome.
2. **Prime:** `n > 1` and no divisor from 2 to $\sqrt{n}$.
3. Combine the two flags to print the message. A fourth case (neither) is also handled.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int n, t, rev = 0, i;
    int isPal, isPrime = 1;

    printf("Enter an integer: ");
    scanf("%d", &n);

    /* palindrome check (negative numbers are not palindromes because of '-') */
    t = n;
    while (t > 0)
    {
        rev = rev * 10 + t % 10;
        t = t / 10;
    }
    isPal = (n >= 0 && rev == n);

    /* prime check */
    if (n < 2)
        isPrime = 0;
    for (i = 2; i * i <= n && isPrime; i++)
        if (n % i == 0)
            isPrime = 0;

    if (isPal && isPrime)
        printf("The number is palindrome and prime both\n");
    else if (isPal)
        printf("The number is palindrome only\n");
    else if (isPrime)
        printf("The number is prime only\n");
    else
        printf("The number is neither palindrome nor prime\n");

    return 0;
}
```

