**Reading the pattern from the example:** for n = 5 the blocks are 5..1, 4..1, 3..1, 2..1, separated by commas. Two recursive functions replace the two loops:
- `block(k, n)` prints k, k-1, ..., 1 (inner "loop").
- `pattern(k, n)` prints block k and then calls `pattern(k-1, n)` (outer "loop"), stopping after the block starting at 2.

A comma is printed before every number except the very first one (k == n in the first block).

```c
#include <stdio.h>

void block(int k, int n, int first)   /* prints k,k-1,...,1 */
{
    if (k == 0)
        return;
    if (!first)
        printf(",");
    printf("%d", k);
    block(k - 1, n, 0);
}

void pattern(int k, int n)            /* blocks k, k-1, ..., 2 */
{
    if (k < 2)
        return;
    block(k, n, k == n);              /* no comma before the very first number */
    pattern(k - 1, n);
}

int main()
{
    int n;
    printf("Enter n: ");
    scanf("%d", &n);
    printf("\n");
    pattern(n, n);
    printf("\n");
    return 0;
}
```

