**What the loop does:** it adds the digits of the number, and if the sum has two digits it adds those digits again, until one digit remains (the **digital root**). For a 3-digit number the first sum is at most 9 + 9 + 9 = 27, so at most two more digit-additions are needed (e.g. 199 to 19 to 10 to 1). These can be written as straight-line statements:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
int main()
{
    int num, sum;
    printf("Enter any 3-digit number\n");
    scanf("%d", &num);
    if (num > 99 && num < 1000)
    {
        sum = num / 100 + (num / 10) % 10 + num % 10;  /* first digit sum (<= 27) */
        sum = sum / 10 + sum % 10;                     /* second pass (<= 10)     */
        sum = sum / 10 + sum % 10;                     /* third pass: one digit   */
        printf("sum = %d", sum);
    }
    return 0;
}
```

Equivalent one-line formula: `sum = 1 + (num - 1) % 9;` (digital root of a positive number).

**Check for 199:** 1 + 9 + 9 = 19, then 1 + 9 = 10, then 1 + 0 = 1. Original loop also prints `sum = 1`.

