**Idea:** scan the prices once, keeping the minimum price seen so far (best day to buy before today). Selling today gives profit `prices[i] - min`; keep the largest.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int n, i, min, maxProfit = 0;
    int prices[100];

    printf("Enter number of days N: ");
    scanf("%d", &n);
    printf("Enter %d prices: ", n);
    for (i = 0; i < n; i++)
        scanf("%d", &prices[i]);

    min = prices[0];
    for (i = 1; i < n; i++) {
        if (prices[i] - min > maxProfit)
            maxProfit = prices[i] - min;   /* sell today */
        if (prices[i] < min)
            min = prices[i];               /* better buy day */
    }

    if (maxProfit > 0)
        printf("%d\n", maxProfit);
    else
        printf("No profit achieved\n");
    return 0;
}
```

**Dry run** for `[102, 6, 49, 3, 100]`:

| Day i | price | min before selling | profit if sold | maxProfit |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 2 | 6 | 102 | -96 | 0 |
| 3 | 49 | 6 | 43 | 43 |
| 4 | 3 | 6 | -3 | 43 |
| 5 | 100 | 3 | 97 | **97** |

Output **97** (buy on day 4 at 3, sell on day 5 at 100). For `[7, 6, 4, 3, 1]` every profit is negative, so the output is "No profit achieved". Buying is always before selling because `min` only uses earlier days. Time $O(N)$.

### Logic
- Read name, roll number and marks of `S1` and `S2` and print them (incorrect results).
- Swap only `name` and `marks`, keeping `roll_no` as it is:
  - Strings cannot be assigned with `=`, so swap names with `strcpy` through a temporary array.
  - Swap marks with a temporary `float`.
- Print the corrected results.

### Program
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

struct student
{
    char name[51];      /* max length 50 + '\0' */
    int roll_no;
    float marks;
};

void show(struct student s)
{
    printf("Name: %-20s Roll no: %-6d Marks: %.2f\n", s.name, s.roll_no, s.marks);
}

int main(void)
{
    struct student S1, S2;
    char tname[51];
    float tmarks;

    printf("Enter name, roll_no, marks of S1: ");
    scanf("%50s %d %f", S1.name, &S1.roll_no, &S1.marks);
    printf("Enter name, roll_no, marks of S2: ");
    scanf("%50s %d %f", S2.name, &S2.roll_no, &S2.marks);

    printf("\nIncorrect results:\n");
    show(S1);
    show(S2);

    /* swap names */
    strcpy(tname, S1.name);
    strcpy(S1.name, S2.name);
    strcpy(S2.name, tname);

    /* swap marks */
    tmarks = S1.marks;
    S1.marks = S2.marks;
    S2.marks = tmarks;

    printf("\nCorrected results:\n");
    show(S1);
    show(S2);
    return 0;
}
```

### Sample run
```
Enter name, roll_no, marks of S1: Rohan 101 72.5
Enter name, roll_no, marks of S2: Simran 102 88

Incorrect results:
Name: Rohan                Roll no: 101    Marks: 72.50
Name: Simran               Roll no: 102    Marks: 88.00

Corrected results:
Name: Simran               Roll no: 101    Marks: 88.00
Name: Rohan                Roll no: 102    Marks: 72.50
```
The roll numbers stay in place; names and marks are exchanged.

