**Formula:**

$$\max(a,b) = \frac{a + b + |a - b|}{2}$$

If $a \ge b$: $\frac{a+b+a-b}{2} = a$. If $a < b$: $\frac{a+b+b-a}{2} = b$. So no comparison operator is needed.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>   /* abs() */

int main(void)
{
    int a, b, max;
    printf("Enter two numbers: ");
    scanf("%d %d", &a, &b);

    /* (a + b + |a - b|) / 2 is always the larger number */
    max = (a + b + abs(a - b)) / 2;

    printf("Maximum = %d\n", max);
    return 0;
}
```

**Sample run:** $a = 8, b = -3$: $\frac{8 + (-3) + 11}{2} = 8$, so `Maximum = 8`.

**Logic:** a 3-digit number reads the same backwards exactly when its hundreds digit equals its units digit (the middle digit stays in place).

- hundreds digit $=$ `n / 100`
- units digit $=$ `n % 10`

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int n, first, last;
    printf("Enter a 3-digit number: ");
    scanf("%d", &n);

    if (n < 100 || n > 999) {
        printf("Not a 3-digit number\n");
        return 0;
    }

    first = n / 100;   /* hundreds digit */
    last  = n % 10;    /* units digit    */

    if (first == last)   /* middle digit always matches itself */
        printf("%d is a palindrome\n", n);
    else
        printf("%d is not a palindrome\n", n);
    return 0;
}
```

**Sample runs:** 121 gives palindrome ($1 = 1$); 123 gives not a palindrome ($1 \ne 3$).

**Method:** `n % 10` gives the last digit and `n / 10` removes it. Repeating this four times (or until n becomes 0) adds every digit.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
int main()
{
    int n, sum = 0;
    printf("Enter a 4-digit number: ");
    scanf("%d", &n);
    if (n < 0) n = -n;              /* digits of -1234 are the same */
    if (n < 1000 || n > 9999) {
        printf("\nNot a 4-digit number\n");
        return 0;
    }
    while (n > 0) {
        sum = sum + n % 10;          /* add last digit */
        n = n / 10;                  /* drop last digit */
    }
    printf("\nSum of digits = %d\n", sum);
    return 0;
}
```

