**Idea:** divide the number by 10 repeatedly; each division removes one digit, so the number of divisions until it becomes 0 is the digit count. The number 0 is handled separately because the loop body would never run for it, yet 0 has 1 digit. A negative sign is not a digit, so we work on the absolute value.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    long long n, t;
    int count = 0;

    printf("Enter an integer: ");
    scanf("%lld", &n);

    t = (n < 0) ? -n : n;      /* sign is not a digit */

    if (t == 0)
        count = 1;             /* 0 is a 1 digit number */

    while (t != 0)
    {
        t = t / 10;            /* remove the last digit */
        count++;
    }

    printf("%lld is a %d digit number\n", n, count);
    return 0;
}
```

**Sample runs**

| Input | Output |
|---|---|
| `0` | `0 is a 1 digit number` |
| `12345` | `12345 is a 5 digit number` |
| `-907` | `-907 is a 3 digit number` |

