**Key point:** `swap(int, int)` receives **copies** of the arguments (call by value). It swaps its own copies, so the values in main are **not** changed.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

void swap(int a, int b)
{
    int temp;
    printf("Inside swap() before swapping: a = %d, b = %d\n", a, b);
    temp = a;
    a = b;
    b = temp;
    printf("Inside swap() after swapping : a = %d, b = %d\n", a, b);
}

int main()
{
    int x, y;
    printf("Enter two integers: ");
    scanf("%d %d", &x, &y);
    printf("\nIn main() before calling swap(): x = %d, y = %d\n", x, y);
    swap(x, y);
    printf("In main() after calling swap() : x = %d, y = %d\n", x, y);
    return 0;
}
```

**Sample run** (input `10 20`):
