**Logic:** two recursive functions replace the two loops.
- `countdown(k)` prints k, k-1, ..., 1 (inner "loop").
- `pattern(n)` calls `countdown(n)` and then `pattern(n-1)` (outer "loop"), until n becomes 0.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

void countdown(int k)          /* prints k k-1 ... 1 */
{
    if (k == 0)
        return;
    printf("%d ", k);
    countdown(k - 1);
}

void pattern(int n)            /* blocks n, n-1, ..., 1 */
{
    if (n == 0)
        return;
    countdown(n);
    pattern(n - 1);
}

int main()
{
    int n;
    printf("Enter n: ");
    scanf("%d", &n);
    printf("\n");
    pattern(n);
    printf("\n");
    return 0;
}
```

**Sample run** (input `5`):
```
Enter n: 
5 4 3 2 1 4 3 2 1 3 2 1 2 1 1 
```
Calls: pattern(5) prints 5 4 3 2 1, pattern(4) prints 4 3 2 1, ..., pattern(1) prints 1, pattern(0) stops.

**Idea:** the array holds $n-1$ distinct numbers from $1$ to $n$. The sum of $1$ to $n$ is $\frac{n(n+1)}{2}$, so

$$\text{missing} = \frac{n(n+1)}{2} - \sum \text{arr}[i]$$

(The first input is the size of the array, $n-1$; e.g. 7 elements means numbers 1 to 8.)

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int size, i;
    long long sum = 0, n, total;
    int arr[1000];

    scanf("%d", &size);              /* size = n - 1 */
    for (i = 0; i < size; i++) {
        scanf("%d", &arr[i]);
        sum += arr[i];
    }
    n = size + 1;                    /* numbers are 1..n */
    total = n * (n + 1) / 2;
    printf("%lld\n", total - sum);
    return 0;
}
```

**Dry run**
- Input 7 and `8 2 4 5 3 7 1`: $n = 8$, total $= 36$, sum $= 30$, output **6**.
- Input 4 and `1 4 5 2`: $n = 5$, total $= 15$, sum $= 12$, output **3**.

Time complexity $O(n)$, no extra array needed for the logic.

