**Idea:** the sign bit of $(a-b)$, shifted right by 31 places (for a 32-bit `int`, arithmetic shift), gives a mask of all 0s when $a \ge b$ and all 1s when $a < b$. AND/OR with this mask selects the right number (assumes $a-b$ does not overflow).

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int a, b, mask, max, min;
    printf("Enter two numbers: ");
    scanf("%d %d", &a, &b);

    /* sign bit of (a - b) spread over all bits:
       mask = 0 (all 0s) if a >= b, -1 (all 1s) if a < b */
    mask = (a - b) >> (sizeof(int) * 8 - 1);

    max = (a & ~mask) | (b & mask);
    min = (b & ~mask) | (a & mask);

    printf("Max = %d\nMin = %d\n", max, min);
    return 0;
}
```

**Sample run:** input `7 12` gives `Max = 12`, `Min = 7`; input `-5 3` gives `Max = 3`, `Min = -5`.

