### Idea
An `int` occupies 4 bytes. The least significant byte (LSB) holds the value `n & 0xFF` (the lowest 8 bits). It can be obtained without any loop in two ways:
1. **Bit masking:** `n & 0xFF` (works on every machine).
2. **Pointer to the first byte:** `unsigned char *p = (unsigned char *)&n;` On a **little-endian** machine (Intel/AMD), the LSB is stored at the lowest address, so `*p` is the LSB. On a big-endian machine the LSB is at `p[sizeof(int) - 1]`.

The character corresponding to that byte is printed with `%c` (its ASCII code is the byte value).

### Program
```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int n, one = 1;
    unsigned char lsb, *p;

    printf("Enter an integer: ");
    scanf("%d", &n);

    /* method 1: masking */
    lsb = n & 0xFF;
    printf("LSB (masking)  = %d, character = '%c'\n", lsb, lsb);

    /* method 2: through a char pointer, checking endianness without a loop */
    p = (unsigned char *)&n;
    if (*(unsigned char *)&one == 1)          /* little-endian */
        lsb = *p;
    else                                       /* big-endian */
        lsb = *(p + sizeof(int) - 1);
    printf("LSB (pointer)  = %d, character = '%c'\n", lsb, lsb);

    return 0;
}
```

### Sample run
Input `16706` (= 0x4142, bytes 42 41 00 00 in little-endian memory):
```
Enter an integer: 16706
LSB (masking)  = 66, character = 'B'
LSB (pointer)  = 66, character = 'B'
```
Here 0x42 = 66, which is the ASCII code of `'B'`.

### Endianness check
`one = 1` is stored as `01 00 00 00` on a little-endian machine, so its first byte read through a `char` pointer is 1; on a big-endian machine it is 0.

