An unsigned 16-bit number has a high byte (bits 15 to 8) and a low byte (bits 7 to 0). Shifting left by 8 moves the low byte up, shifting right by 8 moves the high byte down; OR-ing the two exchanges them.

$$\text{swapped} = ((x \ll 8)\ \&\ \mathrm{0xFF00})\ |\ ((x \gg 8)\ \&\ \mathrm{0x00FF})$$

```c
#include <stdio.h>
int main()
{
    unsigned short x, y;
    printf("Enter an unsigned 16-bit integer: ");
    scanf("%hu", &x);
    y = (unsigned short)(((x << 8) & 0xFF00) | ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF));
    printf("\nBefore: %u (0x%04X)\nAfter : %u (0x%04X)\n", x, x, y, y);
    return 0;
}
```

**Sample run** (input `4660`, i.e. 0x1234):
```
Enter an unsigned 16-bit integer: 
Before: 4660 (0x1234)
After : 13330 (0x3412)
```
The bytes 0x12 and 0x34 have been exchanged to give 0x3412 = 13330.

