Input `0`, `E` prints `0000 1110`.

**Idea:** convert both times to minutes from midnight. `diff = arrival - start`.
- `diff > 0`: Late
- `-30 <= diff <= 0`: On time
- `diff < -30`: Early

If `|diff| >= 1`, print the second line: under 60 minutes as `X minutes`, otherwise as `H:MM hours` with `%02d` for leading zero minutes.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int labHour, labMinute, arrHour, arrMinute;
    int diff, d;

    scanf("%d %d %d %d", &labHour, &labMinute, &arrHour, &arrMinute);

    diff = (arrHour * 60 + arrMinute) - (labHour * 60 + labMinute);

    if (diff > 0)
        printf("Late\n");
    else if (diff >= -30)
        printf("On time\n");
    else
        printf("Early\n");

    d = diff < 0 ? -diff : diff;             /* absolute difference */
    if (d >= 1) {
        if (d < 60)
            printf("%d minutes ", d);
        else
            printf("%d:%02d hours ", d / 60, d % 60);
        printf(diff < 0 ? "before the start\n" : "after the start\n");
    }
    return 0;
}
```

**Dry runs**

| Lab | Arrival | diff (min) | Output |
|---|---|---|---|
| 16:00 | 14:40 | $-80$ | Early / 1:20 hours before the start |
| 16:00 | 15:45 | $-15$ | On time / 15 minutes before the start |
| 16:00 | 16:00 | 0 | On time (no second line) |
| 16:00 | 17:05 | 65 | Late / 1:05 hours after the start |

**Steps:**
1. last = N % 10.
2. Find the first digit and its place value p (the highest power of 10 not above N) by dividing a copy by 10 while it is 10 or more.
3. Remove both digits and put them back swapped:
$$\text{new} = \text{last}\cdot p + (N - \text{first}\cdot p - \text{last}) + \text{first}$$

```c
#include <stdio.h>
int main()
{
    int n, num, first, last, t, sign = 1;
    long p = 1, result;
    scanf("%d", &n);
    num = n;
    if (num < 0) { sign = -1; num = -num; }
    last = num % 10;
    t = num;
    while (t >= 10) {           /* find first digit and its place value */
        t = t / 10;
        p = p * 10;
    }
    first = t;
    result = last * p + (num - first * p - last) + first;
    if (num < 10) result = num; /* single digit: nothing to swap */
    printf("%d\n%ld\n", n, sign * result);
    return 0;
}
```

**Trace for 12345:** last = 5, first = 1, p = 10000. Middle = 12345 - 10000 - 5 = 2340. New = 5 x 10000 + 2340 + 1 = 52341.

**Output:**
```
12345
52341
```

**Condition:** source i balances the rest if $E_i = S - E_i$, i.e. $2E_i = S$ where S is the total. If not, the extra energy needed with source i as the "big" one is $|S - 2E_i|$ (add to source i if it is too small, or to the others if it is too large).

**Why only the maximum is needed:** let M be the largest reading and $R = S - M$ the rest. For any other reading $E \le M$ (with non-negative readings, $E \le R$ too):
$$S - 2E = (M - E) + (R - E) \ \ge\ |R - M|$$
So the best choice is always the maximum, and the answer is $|S - 2M|$. This needs only the running **sum** and **max**, so one loop, no array and no nested loop.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
int main()
{
    int n, i;
    long e, sum = 0, max;
    scanf("%d", &n);
    for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
        scanf("%ld", &e);
        sum += e;
        if (i == 1 || e > max)
            max = e;
    }
    if (2 * max == sum)
        printf("Yes, Sum =%ld\n", max);
    else {
        long need = sum - 2 * max;
        if (need < 0) need = -need;
        printf("No, minimum additional energy required = %ld\n", need);
    }
    return 0;
}
```

**Sample 1** (10, 30, 20): S = 60, M = 30, 2M = 60 = S, so
```
Yes, Sum =30
```

**Sample 2** (10, 25, 20): S = 55, M = 25, $|55 - 50| = 5$, so the output is `No, minimum additional energy required = 5`.

(If negative readings are actually present, the closest $E_i$ to $S/2$ may not be the maximum; then read the values in a recursive function so each $E_i$ is still available on the call stack after S is known, and take the minimum of $|S - 2E_i|$.)

